<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    vst-speechd — the Speech Dispatcher output module.

    A process speech-dispatcher spawns and talks to over stdin and stdout. IT
    LINKS NOTHING: not libspeechd_module, not an audio library, not speechd's
    headers. The module protocol is text, and the audio goes back to the server
    inside it (docs/SPEECHD-PLAN.md, route B) — so there is no per-distro ABI to
    inherit and no build-time dependency on a speech-dispatcher that may be any
    of four versions.

    NativeAOT for the same reason vst-ctl is, only more so. speech-dispatcher
    starts this once per session, but Orca drives it dozens of times a minute and
    the whole design rests on the espeak path answering a keystroke in a few
    milliseconds. A ~100 ms managed startup would not show up here as slowness —
    the process is long-lived — but the .NET runtime would sit resident beside
    the daemon for the whole session, which is the opposite of why the fast voice
    exists.

    InvariantGlobalization because a module inherits whatever environment
    speech-dispatcher was started in, and because the archive must not need ICU —
    which CI learned the hard way on 2026-08-27.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <AssemblyName>vst-speechd</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>VibeSuperTonic.SpeechD</RootNamespace>
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>linux-x64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <PublishAot>true</PublishAot>
    <Version>$(VstVersion)</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\VibeSuperTonic.Core\VibeSuperTonic.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE DAEMON'S PATH POLICY, COMPILED IN RATHER THAN COPIED OUT.

    S3's voice list is read from the models directory, so the module has to
    arrive at the same directory the daemon did or it will report that a machine
    full of voices has none. For a tarball install that is just "beside the
    executable" and any two-line guess would agree; for an AppImage it is the
    store beside the .AppImage file, chosen by a rule with a genuine subtlety in
    it (an existing store beats a preferred location, or moving the file orphans
    383 MB of models).

    It cannot move to Core: R-12 keeps platform path policy out of Core, and
    Core's own csproj states that Core is not allowed to express a platform at
    all. It cannot be re-spelled here either — PiperVoiceInstaller's remarks name
    exactly this failure, two spellings that agree by inspection until the day a
    voice lands somewhere the other one never looks. So it is the same source
    file, the way Onnx.Ort compiles the Engine's SupertonicSdk.cs and RenderHost
    compiles the Launcher's exporters.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\VibeSuperTonic.Daemon\LinuxDataPaths.cs" Link="Shared\LinuxDataPaths.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
